#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""plxnative-lab — receive diagnostic snapshots from PlxNative running in LG Cloud Test Lab.

LG rents us physical televisions and gives us a picture and a virtual remote. It gives us no
console, no ssh, no stdout and no way to download a file, so a bug reproduced on a k8hpp webOS 10
set is a bug the agent fixing it cannot see one log line of. This is the other half of the bridge
described in `docs/lab-diagnostics.md`: a small authenticated HTTPS receiver on the developer's
Mac, reachable from the internet through a TEMPORARY UPnP port mapping on the router, that accepts
exactly one kind of request — the app's own diagnostic upload — and files it where an agent can
read it.

    plxnative-lab start            # session + certificate + listener + port mapping
    plxnative-lab status --json    # what an agent polls
    plxnative-lab logs --follow    # the snapshots, as JSONL on stdout
    plxnative-lab stop             # listener down, mapping deleted

Python 3 standard library only, one file, no dependencies — the idiom `tools/netcond.py` and
`tools/stream-screen.py` already use. `--selftest` proves the whole path against a loopback
transfer with no television and no router in the room.

# The exposed surface, deliberately tiny

For the life of a session `lab.plxnative.com:<port>` is a port on the public internet that reaches
this process. So: ONE route (`POST /v1/diag`), a bearer secret checked before the body is read, a
required and capped `Content-Length`, a socket timeout, a rate limit, a cap on total uploads, no
filesystem serving, no subprocess, and no downward channel of any kind. Everything else is a bare
404. The whole server is a few hundred lines on purpose — it is meant to be read end to end.

# Why a self-signed certificate is the stronger choice here

The alternative is a CA-issued certificate for `lab.plxnative.com`, which means ACME plumbing, a
renewal story and a private key living on a laptop. Instead each session generates its own
certificate and the app pins its public key (`CURLOPT_PINNEDPUBLICKEY`, checked by libcurl
independently of CA verification). The device then trusts exactly one key rather than the
television's entire CA store, no private key ships in the app, and a session's credentials die
with the session.
"""
